{
  "teamId": 136,
  "year": 2023,
  "narrative": "The 2023 Seattle Mariners were a team of dramatic extremes, a club that could look almost unbeatable in one month and completely rudderless the next. After winning the franchise's first playoff series in 2022, expectations were high in Seattle, but a brutal April with a 11-15 record immediately put the team behind the eight ball. Early-season bullpen meltdowns, many of them credited to Matt Brash in the high-leverage data, made it clear that the path to October would not be smooth.\n\nThe turning point came gradually through May, when the offense began to click. Eugenio Suárez provided crucial power, including a stunning three-run homer early in the month, and J.P. Crawford chipped in with clutch extra-base hits. The Mariners went 17-11 in May, then recovered from a difficult June to explode in July and especially August — going a combined 38-15 over those two months. That remarkable summer stretch, fueled by unexpected heroes like Cade Marlowe's grand slam and Dominic Canzone's emerging bat alongside Julio Rodríguez's developing starpower, was the backbone of the entire season.\n\nAugust was arguably the finest month in recent Mariners history, a 21-6 record driven by timely hitting up and down the lineup. Kolten Wong's walk-off home run in late July had lit the fuse, and through August the offense and pitching staff operated in near-perfect harmony. The bullpen, ERA anchored at 3.74 for the full season, kept leads intact night after night while the offense — powered by 210 home runs — gave them plenty to work with.\n\nBut September brought the collapse no Mariners fan wanted to see. A 11-17 record in the final full month saw the bullpen implode at the worst possible moments, with Justin Topa and Tayler Saucedo surrendering pivotal home runs in close games. Julio Rodríguez's 30th home run on September 11th was a milestone moment lost in the broader heartbreak of a fading wild card race. J.P. Crawford's dramatic three-run double on September 28th was too little, too late. Seattle finished 88-74, a strong record on paper, but good enough only to watch October from home — a painful reminder that in baseball, timing is everything.",
  "chapters": [
    {
